Below is a full, end-to-end, production-grade explanation of Rate Limiting in Spring Cloud Gateway (SCG) — from why it exists → internal architecture → exact implementation steps → real production pitfalls.

I’ll explain it as if you were building a Zepto-scale API Gateway.

---

# 1️⃣ What is Rate Limiting (Conceptually)

Rate limiting = controlling how many requests a client can make in a given time window

Examples:

 100 requests / minute / user
 10 requests / second / IP
 5 checkout requests / minute

### Why it’s mandatory

Without rate limiting:

 ❌ DDoS can kill your system
 ❌ One bad client can exhaust threads
 ❌ Downstream services get overloaded
 ❌ Retry storms amplify failures

---

# 2️⃣ Where Rate Limiting Lives in Microservices

### Correct placement

```
Client
  ↓
API Gateway  ←── Rate Limiting (HERE)
  ↓
Microservices
```

Why Gateway?

 Single choke point
 No duplication
 Protects all downstream services

---

# 3️⃣ Rate Limiting in Spring Cloud Gateway (High Level)

Spring Cloud Gateway uses a Filter-based model.

```
Incoming Request
   ↓
Global Filters
   ↓
Route Filters (RateLimiter filter here)
   ↓
Forward to Service
```

Rate limiting is implemented via:

```
GatewayFilter → RateLimiter → Redis
```

---

# 4️⃣ Core Components Involved (VERY IMPORTANT)

### 1️⃣ GatewayFilter

Intercepts request before routing.

### 2️⃣ RateLimiter Interface

```java
public interface RateLimiter<C> {
    Mono<Response> isAllowed(String routeId, String key);
}
```

### 3️⃣ KeyResolver

Decides WHO is being rate limited.

Examples:

 IP address
 User ID
 API key
 JWT claim

### 4️⃣ Redis

Stores counters atomically.

⚠️ Redis is mandatory in production
(In-memory rate limiting is unsafe in distributed systems)

---

# 5️⃣ Algorithm Used: Token Bucket (Default)

SCG uses Token Bucket algorithm

### Token Bucket explained

 Bucket has capacity (burst)
 Tokens refill at a fixed rate
 Each request consumes 1 token
 If no token → ❌ 429 Too Many Requests

---

### Parameters

| Property        | Meaning                        |
| --------------- | ------------------------------ |
| replenishRate   | Tokens added per second        |
| burstCapacity   | Max bucket size                |
| requestedTokens | Tokens per request (usually 1) |

